course: dictionary
lesson: 287
title: Amount: how much, and what it adds up to
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An amount is how much there is of something. You can have a small amount of time, a large amount of water, or an amount of money. Read the words around amount to see which kind of amount is meant.
explain: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» Here the amounts are the totals of money from each kind of coin. «The amount of water on Earth stays about the same» shows that amount can also mean the quantity of one thing.
explain: Amount can also be a verb: to amount to something means to add up to a total, or to be the same as it. Try the swap test: put how much or add up to into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: In a glass or a bowl, the juice changes shape, but «keeps the same amount». The shape changes, but the amount of juice does not.
fact: An amount is how much there is of something.
fact: «A large amount» means a lot; «a small amount» means only a little.
fact: To amount to something means to add up to a total, or to be the same as it.
fact: «Noses can sense tiny amounts» of hydrogen sulphide gas, which is why rotten eggs smell.
fact: The amount of water on Earth stays about the same; it just keeps moving around.
exercise: In «noses can sense tiny amounts of it», what does amount mean? | A shape like a glass | A bowl of soup | A loud noise | *How much of the gas there is
exercise: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» What are the amounts? | The names printed on the coins | The sizes of the coins | *Totals of money from each kind of coin | The shapes of the coins
exercise: The juice takes the shape of a tall glass or a wide bowl, but what stays the same? | The shape | The glass | *The amount | The bowl
test: «A small amount» of time means what? | A lot of time | *Only a little time | The same time as yesterday | Time that has stopped
test: «The amount of water on Earth stays about the same.» What does this tell us? | Water grows bigger each day | Water only exists in rivers | The water is all gone | *The water keeps moving around but the total stays about the same
exam: In «The bill will amount to ten pounds», what does amount to mean? | Paint over | *Add up to a total of | Forget about | Shrink down to
